 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050717 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050436 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050436 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186175892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050436 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186175893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050436 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186175894 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050436 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186175895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050436 #edb/ref 1031 "Rounding Up Decimals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050436 #edb/ref 1033 3585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050436 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "490bb9e7-cb49-5d04-a7c1-aff95960778a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050469 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050469 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050469 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050469 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050469 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050469 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050469 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186083175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050469 #edb/ref 1031 "The Common Logarithm" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050469 #edb/ref 1033 726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050469 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "4c3afa77-0505-5a1a-9d73-711526ff20ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050792 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050792 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050792 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050792 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050792 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050792 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050792 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050792 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050792 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050792 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050792 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050792 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050792 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186188302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050792 #edb/ref 1031 "Units of Area and Volume" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050792 #edb/ref 1033 3872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050792 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "675d7c57-7050-5b6e-8c59-6608d72d4947" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050934 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050934 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050934 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050934 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050934 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050934 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050934 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186073875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050934 #edb/ref 1031 "Dividing Decimals Using the Standard Algorithm" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050934 #edb/ref 1033 549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050934 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "72a4f3fd-74ae-5991-918c-b3f68b458c76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138164 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14084" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138164 #edb/ref 1071 "The height $h$ of a lamppost is $30\\,\\textrm{ft}$ to the nearest $1\\,\\textrm{ft}$. Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138164 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b18fef84-bbbb-58c6-a215-4146461c28f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138164 #edb/ref 1074 "Our measurement is given to the nearest $1\\,\\textrm{ft}$. So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1\\,\\textrm{ft}}{2} = 0.5\\,\\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlower bound &= 30ft - 0.5ft \\\\\n&= 29.5ft \\\\\nupper bound &= 30ft + 0.5ft \\\\\n&= 30.5ft\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $h$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n29.5\\,\\textrm{ft} \\leq h < 30.5\\,\\textrm{ft}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138165 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14085" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138165 #edb/ref 1071 "The area $a$ of a golf course measures $0.6\\,\\textrm{km}^2$ to the nearest tenth of a square kilometer. Determine the upper and lower bounds of this measurement." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138165 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e0f0485-0635-558e-8493-eef61ee623f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138165 #edb/ref 1074 "Our measurement is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\textrm{km}^2$. So, the greatest possible error of this measurement is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.1\\,\\textrm{km}^2}{2} = 0.05\\,\\textrm{km}^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the lower and upper bounds of this measurement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlower bound &= 0.6km^{2} - 0.05km^{2} \\\\\n&= 0.55km^{2} \\\\\nupper bound &= 0.6km^{2} + 0.05km^{2} \\\\\n&= 0.65km^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the exact value of $a$ lies between the lower and upper bound, we conclude that\n\n$$\n0.55\\,\\textrm{km}^2 \\leq a < 0.65\\,\\textrm{km}^2\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138167 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14087" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138167 #edb/ref 1071 "The dimensions of a parking lot are $90\\, \\textrm{m} \\times 50\\, \\textrm{m}$, where each dimension is measured to the nearest $10\\, \\textrm{m}$. Rounded to the nearest ten square meters, what is the upper bound for the area of the parking lot?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138167 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "44ff13ac-1980-554e-aea8-4aaa51d202fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138167 #edb/ref 1074 "Each dimension is given to the nearest $10\\,\\textrm{m}$. So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10\\,\\textrm{m}}{2} = 5\\,\\textrm{m}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *upper bound* for the area of the parking lot. Therefore, we require the *upper bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe upper bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n90m + 5m = 95m; 50m + 5m = 55m\n$$\n\nSo, the upper bound for the area of the parking lot is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= 95m \\cdot 55m \\\\\n&= 5225 \\\\\n&\\approx 5230m^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest ten square meters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138387 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138387 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138387 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138387 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138387 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138387 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138387 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Bounds When Rounding to a Whole Number" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138387 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138387 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "61a5c363-0371-5820-a4e4-b21335406e50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138388 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138388 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138388 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138753 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138388 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding Bounds When Rounding to a Decimal Place" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138388 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138388 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e99cabd4-b713-50e1-83be-f8571b575803" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138389 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138389 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138389 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138389 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138389 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186138801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138389 #edb/ref 1039 "Bounding Areas and Volumes" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138389 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186138167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138389 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "29217571-4597-5bbe-aecd-0d9a1d1aa1c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186139298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1045 "Degrees of Accuracy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186139294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138593 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "88d9417f-4dcb-5b07-99a4-4fc3631a5617" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139294 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139294 #edb/ref 1061 165169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139294 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cc0c262d-dd9e-52a1-a83b-9f1c0fe96262" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139294 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139295 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139295 #edb/ref 1061 165170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139295 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "66ba1355-ab27-5d81-8157-a359b9801285" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139295 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139296 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139296 #edb/ref 1061 165171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139296 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bf5aa060-ae70-531a-8e1c-d165c1d25adb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139296 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139297 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139297 #edb/ref 1061 165174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139297 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7d898aa1-1cb4-5977-87b3-02ae7082146a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139297 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139298 #edb/ref 1061 165177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139298 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "761a41b0-39e8-54bb-9403-886bbe9ed521" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139298 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139715 #edb/ref 1064 "We always introduce **errors** in our measurements whenever we round or approximate numbers. In this lesson, we will explore how to quantify and describe measurement errors.\n\nSuppose that a particular rope measures precisely $48.9\\,\\textrm{cm}$. Now, imagine we take a meterstick and record a measurement of $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$ *to the nearest $10$ centimeters*. Let's mark this approximation on our meterstick.\n\n![](images/4e/4e5d5ca05f27aa612b3fdcc9430b495870ddf540630857b6227897d6b20c9e89.png)\n\nThe error of a measurement equals the difference between the rounded estimate and the \"true\" value. So, in this example, the error of our approximation equals\n\n$$\n50\\,\\textrm{cm} - 48.9\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1.1\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nWe often don't know the \"true\" measurement in practical problems. So, the next best thing is to determine a **lower bound** and an **upper bound** for our measurement:\n\n- The *lower* bound of a measurement is its *smallest* possible value.\n- The *upper* bound of a measurement is its *largest* possible value.\n\nLet's figure out the lower and upper bounds of our $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$ approximation:\n\n- The *lower bound* is $45 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ because this is the *smallest* value that gives $50 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ when rounded to the nearest $10\\,\\textrm{cm}$.\n\n![](images/da/da9cd6208cef81c6fe34fbba9b4271fa3ce1f30df69eb3c631ce6fea4f12e3a3.png)\n\n- The *upper bound* is $55 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ because this is (almost) the *largest* value that gives $50 \\, \\textrm{cm}$ when rounded to the nearest $10\\,\\textrm{cm}$.\n\n![](images/23/23a195d88326b587e1864ef04e7b05f74d675f1ddc987baa2cf586af599eb4a9.png)\n\nLet's use the letter $l$ to denote the \"true\" length of the rope. Then, we can combine our lower and upper bound measurements into a single statement, as follows:\n\n$$\n45\\,\\textrm{cm} \\leq l \\lt 55\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used a *strict* \"<\" inequality for the upper bound. We cannot include $55\\textrm{cm}$ in our set of values because this number rounds to $60\\,\\textrm{cm}$, not $50\\,\\textrm{cm}$.\n\nWe can visualize the lower and upper bounds on our diagram as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fdacb0787e8316a78da1ad65aa177e9a6f29215fa0d53aef88b0eb33326218d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139715 #edb/ref 1065 8751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139715 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d44fcde5-ad8a-596b-a6ac-955ff40b6bd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139715 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139716 #edb/ref 1064 "So, we've seen how to calculate lower and upper bounds. Before we consider another example, note the following:\n\n- The lower and upper bound depend on our approximation's **degree of accuracy**.\n- In the last example, the degree of accuracy was $10\\,\\textrm{cm}$ because the rope was measured to the nearest $10$ centimeters.\n- If we change the degree of accuracy, the lower and upper bounds will also change. So, for example, if we measured the rope to the nearest $1\\,\\textrm{cm}$, the lower and upper bounds would be different.\n- The **greatest possible error** is the largest amount the \"true\" value can be from the approximation.\n- This greatest possible error is always one-half of the degree of accuracy. So, in the last example, the greatest possible error in our measurement was\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10\\,\\textrm{cm}}{2} = {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}}\n$$\n\nWe can use the greatest possible error to compute the lower and upper bounds quickly:\n\n- To compute the lower bound of an approximation, we *subtract* the greatest possible error from the approximation. So, for the previous example, we have\n$\\textrm{lower bound} = 50\\,\\textrm{cm} - {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}} = 45\\,\\textrm{cm.}$\n- To compute the upper bound of an approximation, we *add* the greatest possible error to the approximation. So, for the previous example, we have\n$\\textrm{upper bound} = 50\\,\\textrm{cm} + {\\color{blue}{5\\,\\textrm{cm}}} = 55\\,\\textrm{cm.}$\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139716 #edb/ref 1065 8752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139716 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f1808067-d542-577c-a2d3-085e0fb1df59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139716 #edb/ref 1067 "The Greatest Possible Error" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
